From: Mihawk <163614687+mihawk-99@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 13 Oct 2026 17:00:00 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] ntoskrnl, ps5: a device interface takes over its link, whatever
 is left behind

On the PS5 every program runs its own HID stack on the title's one
wineserver (patch 0870) and the DualSense's interface link has one name for
all of them. A link outlives the process that made it. Patch 0941 meant a
program's bus to replace such a link when IoCreateSymbolicLink answered
STATUS_OBJECT_NAME_EXISTS, but the server's create_symlink clears that
status when it opens an existing link, so the old link stayed and the code
that replaced it never ran (0941 itself listed it as not exercised).

The first session of a new prefix showed it. Wine runs the Mono
installer's tools while it builds the prefix; one of them started a bus,
made the link and ended. The game's bus then found its interface listed and
enabled in 32 ms, and every open of it failed with "path not found": the
link led to \Device\HID#ac#..., a device of the process that was gone
(probe run hid-enum-fresh-2026-10-13-02: CreateFile error 3; the link's
target opened with status c0000034, on every one of 30 rounds). SDL never
found the DualSense and a .NET game ignored the pad, while XInput read 103
presses in the user's own run. The same happens whenever a program that
made a bus ends before the next one starts, in any session.

IoCreateSymbolicLink's answer is not used any more: the interface deletes
the link of its name first, then makes its own, so the name always leads to
the newest program's device ("a program's DualSense is its own", 0941).
---
diff --git a/dlls/ntoskrnl.exe/pnp.c b/dlls/ntoskrnl.exe/pnp.c
--- a/dlls/ntoskrnl.exe/pnp.c
+++ b/dlls/ntoskrnl.exe/pnp.c
@@ -883,17 +883,18 @@
     }
 
     RtlInitUnicodeString( &device_nameU, device_name );
-    ret = IoCreateSymbolicLink( symlink_name, &device_nameU );
-    if (ret == STATUS_OBJECT_NAME_EXISTS)
-    {
-        /* A link of this name is already there, made by a driver stack that ended without removing it: a link is
-         * permanent, so it outlives the process that made it. On the PS5 every program runs its own HID stack on
-         * the title's one server (patch 0870); wineboot's DualSense left its link, the game's DualSense found the
-         * name taken, its interface stayed disabled, and no program in the game could find the controller
-         * (2026-10-06). The name belongs to this device now. */
+    /* A link of this name may be there already, made by a driver stack that ended without removing it: a link is
+     * permanent, so it outlives the process that made it. On the PS5 every program runs its own HID stack on the
+     * title's one server (patch 0870), and the name belongs to the program that enabled the interface last, so
+     * this device takes it over. Patch 0941 meant to do that when IoCreateSymbolicLink answered
+     * STATUS_OBJECT_NAME_EXISTS, but the server clears that status (create_symlink opens the existing link and
+     * reports success), so the old link stayed, still leading to a device that was gone. In the first session of a
+     * new prefix the Mono installer's tools had made one before the game started: the game's interface was listed
+     * and enabled, every open of it failed with "path not found", and SDL never found the DualSense (probe run
+     * hid-enum-fresh-2026-10-13-02: link target \Device\HID#ac#..., opening it status c0000034). */
+    if (!IoDeleteSymbolicLink( symlink_name ))
         MESSAGE( "[PS5 Proton] %s: replacing a link left behind\n", debugstr_us( symlink_name ) );
-        if (!(ret = IoDeleteSymbolicLink( symlink_name ))) ret = IoCreateSymbolicLink( symlink_name, &device_nameU );
-    }
+    ret = IoCreateSymbolicLink( symlink_name, &device_nameU );
     free( device_name );
     return ret;
 }
-- 
